#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
truenas_config_dr.py — build disaster-recovery documentation from a TrueNAS
configuration export, entirely offline.

Why the config file instead of the API
--------------------------------------
* No API key, no network, no live session against the NAS.
* Runs on an air-gapped workstation: the NAS never knows it happened.
* The export is the exact artifact you restore from, so the document
  describes precisely what a restore will bring back.

Input
-----
Download from the TrueNAS UI: System > General Settings > Manage Configuration
> Download File, and tick "Export Password Secret Seed". That gives a .tar
holding freenas-v1.db (SQLite) and pwenc_secret (the key TrueNAS uses to
encrypt stored passwords). A bare .db works too, but encrypted fields stay
opaque without the seed.

Security model
--------------
* The database is loaded straight into memory; it is never extracted to disk
  (Python 3.11+; on older Pythons a 0600 temp copy in /dev/shm is used and
  removed immediately).
* The database is opened read-only (query_only).
* Default output is REDACTED: secrets show as [set]/[empty]. Anything TrueNAS
  itself stores encrypted is redacted unless its name is on a short
  known-harmless list (host, port, username, region, ...).
* Unredacted output needs an explicit confirmation (type the hostname) and
  gets a warning banner plus an _UNREDACTED filename tag. Encrypted fields
  can only be shown if the export includes the secret seed.
* Output files are created 0600 via a private temp file and atomic rename.
  Optionally they are piped straight into GPG so plaintext never touches disk.
* Secrets are never printed to the terminal.

Known limit of redaction: free-text fields (cron commands, init scripts, SMB
auxiliary parameters, tunables) are shown as-is. If you embed a token or
password in a cron command line, it will appear in the redacted document.

Requirements: Python 3.8+. Optional: python3-cryptography (decrypting secret
fields, certificate expiry dates), gpg (encrypted output).
On Linux Mint:  sudo apt install python3-cryptography

Usage
-----
  python3 truenas_config_dr.py                       # interactive wizard
  python3 truenas_config_dr.py -c backup.tar --batch # redacted md+html, defaults
  python3 truenas_config_dr.py -c backup.tar --batch --unredacted \
          --confirm-host faultline --gpg-recipient you@example.com
"""

from __future__ import annotations

